Pressure gradients, separation and integral methods (@fd-pressure-gradient: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Intermediate-Fluid-Mechanics-Liburdy.lingua#r_45KKWKB7DTN9MMQ951P62JHH5N", #source "../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA", #source "../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P", #fluid-dynamics "Jets-wakes-entrainment-and-free-shear-layers.lingua#r_50ENQJKN158A435N37YDR84562") { r_6SZR7KMJ0QJHB12ZVVGMZ254WA
**Read:** [Intermediate Fluid Mechanics — James Liburdy](../sources/Intermediate-Fluid-Mechanics-Liburdy.lingua#r_45KKWKB7DTN9MMQ951P62JHH5N), chapter IX in full; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), Falkner–Skan and separation; [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), chapter 3 pressure-gradient and momentum-integral notes.

An adverse streamwise pressure gradient decelerates the outer flow and modifies the slower near-wall fluid strongly. In the simple steady two-dimensional boundary-layer picture, separation is associated with wall shear reaching zero and reverse flow developing. General unsteady or three-dimensional separation requires a more careful definition than this single scalar criterion.

Integrate the momentum equation to obtain dθ/dx+(2+H)(θ/U_e)dU_e/dx=C_f/2, where H=δ*/θ and C_f uses local U_e. The equation is exact within the boundary-layer approximation but needs additional information to determine profile shape and wall shear. An assumed profile or empirical closure therefore carries physical content and uncertainty.

Work one favorable and one adverse pressure-gradient case, comparing an integral approximation with a differential solution or primary measurements. Study the Falkner–Skan similarity family and the restrictions on its solutions rather than extrapolating flat-plate constants. Explain how separation changes downstream pressure, drag and the validity of a one-way outer-flow/boundary-layer coupling.

**Continue:** [Jets, wakes, entrainment and free shear layers](Jets-wakes-entrainment-and-free-shear-layers.lingua#r_50ENQJKN158A435N37YDR84562).

